UND = {
 1: {
  0: 'Every origin theory answers one question: how did a cloud of gas and dust turn into a star with planets round it? Read them as a chain where each new theory repairs the weak point of the one before it, ending with the <b>Big Bang</b> for the universe as a whole. For each, hold three things: who gave it, the idea in one line, and its drawback or proof.',
  1: 'What we can see (stars, gas, planets) is only a small part of the universe. <b>Dark matter</b> is the unseen mass that holds galaxies together by gravity; <b>dark energy</b> is the unseen push that makes the universe expand faster. One pulls in, the other pushes out.',
  2: 'A <b>galaxy</b> is a huge family of stars, gas and dust held together by gravity. They are sorted by shape, so learn the shapes with one example each and where our own Milky Way fits.',
  3: 'A star lives by burning hydrogen. Its <b>mass decides its death</b>: a small star ends quietly, a heavy one explodes and leaves a very dense remnant. Follow the life line once from nebula to the end states and the terms fall into place.',
  4: 'The solar system is the Sun plus everything its gravity holds: planets, their moons and the small bodies. Exam questions here are about telling the <b>small bodies apart</b> (asteroid, comet, meteor), so learn what each is made of and where it lives.',
  5: 'Two families: the <b>inner</b> rocky planets close to the Sun and the <b>outer</b> giant planets beyond the asteroid belt. Compare them feature by feature, then keep one or two special facts for each planet.',
  6: 'Think of the Sun as layers, from the core where energy is made to the outer atmosphere we see only in an eclipse. Its outbursts (flares, solar wind, storms) matter because of what they do on Earth: auroras and trouble for satellites, radio and power grids.',
  7: 'The Earth began hot and molten. As it cooled, heavy material sank and light material rose, so it became <b>layered</b>; gases from inside made the first atmosphere, and rain filled the oceans; life then changed the air by adding oxygen. One sequence: layers, air, water, life.',
  8: 'Three ideas: the Earth’s size and shape figures, the Earth as a <b>magnet</b> (and why that protects us), and the two motions, <b>rotation</b> (day and night) and <b>revolution</b> with a tilted axis (seasons, solstices, equinoxes).',
  9: 'For the Moon, learn how it is thought to have formed, why we always see the <b>same face</b>, and how its phases follow from where it stands relative to the Sun and Earth.',
  10: 'An eclipse is just a shadow: Sun, Earth and Moon fall in one line. <b>Latitude</b> tells how far north or south (and so climate), <b>longitude</b> tells how far east or west (and so time). Time questions are simple arithmetic once you know which way the Earth turns.',
  11: 'Nobody has seen the inside of the Earth; we infer it, mainly from how <b>earthquake waves</b> bend and stop. The result is three layers by composition (crust, mantle, core) with sharp boundaries (discontinuities) between them.',
  12: 'An earthquake is stored strain released suddenly at the <b>focus</b>; the point above it is the <b>epicentre</b>. The energy travels as body waves (P and S) and surface waves, and the way each wave behaves tells us about the interior and creates <b>shadow zones</b>.',
  13: 'Two sets of forces shape the land. <b>Endogenic</b> forces from inside build relief up (slow uplift, or sudden quakes and volcanoes). <b>Exogenic</b> forces from outside wear it down: weathering breaks rock where it stands, erosion carries it away.',
  14: 'Wegener said the continents moved (fit of coasts, fossils, rocks) but could not say what moved them. Later evidence from the <b>sea floor</b> (young rock at ridges, magnetic stripes) showed new crust forming and spreading. Drift gave the idea, sea-floor spreading gave the mechanism.',
  15: 'The outer shell is broken into rigid <b>plates</b> that ride on the softer layer below. Everything interesting happens at their edges: they move apart, come together or slide past, and each kind of edge makes its own landforms, quakes and volcanoes.',
  16: 'A volcano is an opening where <b>magma</b> reaches the surface. How runny the magma is decides everything: runny lava spreads quietly into broad shields and plateaus, sticky lava traps gas and explodes into steep cones. Then learn the intrusive forms that cool inside the crust.',
  17: 'Mountains are sorted by <b>how they were made</b>: squeezed into folds, lifted or dropped as blocks along faults, piled up by volcanoes, or left standing after erosion. <b>Isostasy</b> is the balance idea: higher mountains have deeper roots, like a bigger iceberg sitting deeper in water (memory aid).',
  18: 'Three rock families by origin: <b>igneous</b> (cooled from melt), <b>sedimentary</b> (laid down in layers) and <b>metamorphic</b> (changed by heat and pressure). The rock cycle only says that any one can become any other.',
  19: 'Running water is the main sculptor of land. A river <b>cuts</b> in its upper course, <b>carries</b> in the middle and <b>drops</b> its load in the lower course, and each stage leaves its own landforms. Davis read this as youth, maturity and old age.',
  20: '<b>Karst</b> is what happens when slightly acidic water dissolves limestone: holes and caves below, sinks and dry valleys above, and dripstone features inside the caves.',
  21: 'Waves do the same two jobs as rivers: they <b>erode</b> cliffs and headlands and <b>deposit</b> beaches, bars and spits. Coasts are then classed by whether the land sank (submergence) or rose (emergence).',
  22: 'A glacier is a slow river of ice. It <b>plucks and grinds</b> the rock in the mountains (erosional forms, U-shaped valleys) and <b>dumps</b> unsorted debris lower down (moraines and other depositional forms).',
  23: 'In deserts, <b>wind</b> and rare flash floods do the work. Wind removes loose material, sand-blasts rock and piles sand into dunes. Lakes and plateaus are then learnt simply by how they were formed.',
 },
 2: {
  0: 'The atmosphere is a stack of layers, sorted by how <b>temperature changes with height</b>. Weather stays in the lowest layer; ozone warms the next one. Learn each layer with its height, its temperature trend and the one thing it is known for.',
  1: 'The Earth’s surface is heated by the Sun and the air is heated mostly <b>from below</b> by the ground. Over a year the heat coming in equals the heat going out (heat budget). <b>Albedo</b> is simply how much sunlight a surface throws back.',
  2: 'Normally air gets colder as you go up. In an <b>inversion</b> a warm layer sits above cold air like a lid, so the air below cannot rise: fog and smoke are trapped, but frost-sensitive crops on slopes may be spared.',
  3: 'Air has weight, so it presses. Heated air rises and leaves <b>low pressure</b>; cold or sinking air gives <b>high pressure</b>. Wind is air moving from high to low, turned by the Earth’s rotation (<b>Coriolis</b>). That alone gives the pressure belts and the three wind systems.',
  4: 'High up, away from friction, the pressure push and Coriolis balance, so wind blows <b>along</b> the isobars: the geostrophic wind. <b>Jet streams</b> are narrow, very fast rivers of such air near the top of the troposphere.',
  5: '<b>Local winds</b> are small-scale and named: they blow because land and sea, or valley and slope, heat and cool at different rates, or because air warms as it comes down a mountain. <b>Monsoon</b> is the same land-sea idea at the scale of a continent and a season.',
  6: 'Warm air can hold more water vapour. Cool it enough (<b>dew point</b>) and vapour condenses on tiny particles: near the ground it is dew, fog or frost, higher up it is <b>cloud</b>. Clouds are named by height and shape; rain is classed by what lifted the air.',
  7: 'An <b>air mass</b> is a large body of air that has taken on the temperature and moisture of the region it sat over. Where two unlike air masses meet, the boundary is a <b>front</b>, and the lighter warm air is forced up, giving cloud and rain.',
  8: 'A cyclone is air spiralling into a <b>low-pressure centre</b>. <b>Tropical</b> cyclones are heat engines fed by warm sea water; <b>temperate</b> cyclones are born on fronts where warm and cold air meet. Learn the two side by side.',
  9: 'Köppen sorted climates using only <b>temperature and rainfall</b>, with letter codes. Climatic-region questions give a description and ask you to name the type, so for each type hold its location, rainfall pattern and natural vegetation.',
 },
 3: {
  0: 'Think of walking into the sea from the beach. First a gentle, shallow step (the <b>shelf</b>), then a steep staircase (the <b>slope</b>), a soft landing of sediment (the <b>rise</b>), a wide flat floor (the <b>abyssal plain</b>) and, where one plate dives under another, a deep cellar (the <b>trench</b>). <i>The house picture is only a memory aid.</i> Learn each part with its gradient, depth and one example.',
  1: 'Three properties decide how sea water behaves: how <b>warm</b> it is, how <b>salty</b> it is and so how <b>heavy</b> (dense) it is. Sun and rain set the first two at the surface: hot, dry belts give warm and very salty water; rain, rivers and melting ice dilute it. Cold and salty water is heavier and sinks, and that sinking is what drives the deep currents of the next topic.',
  2: 'Two PYQs test one idea: almost all of Earth’s water is salty, and most of the small fresh share is locked in <b>ice</b>. Of what is left, <b>groundwater</b> is far bigger than all rivers and lakes together. Read each statement against that order: ice, then groundwater, then surface water.',
  3: 'A current is a river inside the ocean that flows in one direction all year. <b>Wind</b> pushes the top layer, the Earth’s spin (<b>Coriolis</b>) turns it and the continents block it, so the water goes round in big loops called <b>gyres</b>. Below, cold salty water sinks at the poles and creeps along the bottom (<b>thermohaline</b>). Western sides of oceans get warm, fast currents; eastern sides get cold, slow ones with upwelling.',
  4: '<b>Tides</b> are the sea rising and falling twice a day because the Moon (mainly) and the Sun pull the water, with the Earth’s centrifugal force making the bulge on the far side. When Sun, Moon and Earth are in one line the pull adds up (<b>spring</b>); at right angles it partly cancels (<b>neap</b>). <b>Waves</b> are different: wind passes energy through the water while the water itself only circles in place.',
  5: 'The <b>cryosphere</b> is all the ice on Earth. It acts as a freshwater store, a mirror (albedo) and a lid over trapped gases. When it melts, each of those jobs fails: water supply changes, more heat is absorbed, gases escape and ocean circulation shifts.',
  6: 'A coral is a tiny animal (polyp) living with an alga, <b>zooxanthellae</b>, inside it. The alga makes food from sunlight, so reefs need warm, clear, shallow, low-nutrient water. Under stress, mainly heat, the coral throws the alga out and turns white: <b>bleaching</b>.',
  7: 'Whatever settles on the sea floor is sorted by <b>where it came from</b>: land, living things, chemical precipitation from sea water, or space. Four sources, four names.',
  8: 'A <b>dead zone</b> is water with too little oxygen for most life. The chain is always the same: nutrients run off the land, algae bloom, the algae die and sink, and bacteria use up the oxygen while decomposing them.',
  9: 'The ocean gives living resources (fish, seaweed, pearls) and non-living ones (oil and gas, energy, salts, nodules). The best fishing grounds sit where a <b>warm and a cold current meet</b>, because the mixing feeds plankton.',
  10: '<b>UNCLOS 1982</b> divides the sea into belts measured outward from the coast: the closer the belt, the stronger the coastal State’s rights. Remember the three distances, 12, 24 and 200 nautical miles, and what the State may do in each.',
 },
 4: {
  0: 'Fix India on the globe first: its latitudes and longitudes, the Tropic of Cancer cutting it in half, the standard meridian and its size and neighbours. Most other facts (climate, time, coastline) follow from this position.',
  1: 'India has six physical units. Start with the <b>Himalayas</b>: young fold mountains in parallel ranges from north to south, and also divided west to east by river gaps. Learn the ranges, then the regional divisions, passes and peaks on top of that frame.',
  2: 'The Northern Plains are the <b>fill</b>: sediment brought by the Indus, Ganga and Brahmaputra systems into the trough south of the Himalayas. From the foothills outward the belts change with the size of the deposit: coarse first, fine later.',
  3: 'The Thar lies in the rain shadow of the Aravallis. Learn its dry landforms and its one inland drainage story.',
  4: 'The Peninsular Plateau is the <b>old, stable block</b> of India: a tilted tableland of hard rock, bounded by hill ranges, higher in the west and sloping east, which is why most of its big rivers flow to the Bay of Bengal.',
  5: 'Compare the two coasts as a pair: the <b>western</b> one is narrow, with estuaries and backwaters; the <b>eastern</b> one is broad, with deltas. Learn the local names section by section.',
  6: 'Two island groups with different origins: the <b>Andaman and Nicobar</b> group is the top of a submerged mountain chain (with volcanoes), the <b>Lakshadweep</b> group is built of coral. Channels and the named points are the usual exam targets.',
  8: 'Drainage is the pattern rivers make. Two families: <b>Himalayan</b> rivers (snow-fed, perennial, long, still cutting) and <b>Peninsular</b> rivers (rain-fed, seasonal, old and settled). Learn the vocabulary first, then each system with source, course and tributaries.',
  9: 'For every river hold the same five things: <b>source, course, right-bank and left-bank tributaries, and mouth</b>. The Indus system is tested through its five Punjab tributaries and their order.',
  10: 'The Ganga is built from headstreams that join one by one at the <b>prayags</b>, then collects Himalayan tributaries from the left and both Himalayan and Peninsular ones from the right. Bank-wise lists are the exam favourite.',
  11: 'The Brahmaputra changes its <b>name</b> along its course and brings a very heavy load of silt, which makes it braided and flood-prone. The Barak is the separate system to its south.',
  12: 'Peninsular rivers split by direction: most flow <b>east</b> into the Bay of Bengal and build deltas; the Narmada and Tapi flow <b>west</b> through rift valleys and form estuaries. Then inland drainage, river-linking and lakes.',
  14: 'India’s climate is the <b>monsoon</b> rhythm: winds reverse with the season. Learn the controls first, then the four seasons in order, and then the ocean and atmosphere patterns (El Niño, IOD and others) that strengthen or weaken the monsoon.',
  15: 'Forest type follows <b>rainfall</b> (and height in the mountains): wettest areas carry evergreen forest, moderate rain gives deciduous, dry areas thorn, coasts mangrove, and high altitudes alpine. Learn each with rainfall, region and typical trees.',
  16: 'A soil is its <b>parent rock plus climate plus time</b>. Each Indian soil type is learnt the same way: where it occurs, how it formed, its colour and texture, what it is rich or poor in, and the crops it suits.',
 },
 5: {
  0: 'Demography studies <b>how many people, where, and how fast the number changes</b>. Distribution is uneven because of land, climate, water and jobs; the key terms are simple ratios, so know exactly what each measures.',
  1: 'A population pyramid is a bar chart of age groups, males on one side and females on the other. Its <b>shape</b> tells the story: a wide base means many children and a growing population, a narrow base means an ageing one.',
  2: 'Malthus argued that population grows faster than food supply, so shortage must follow unless growth is checked. Learn his two kinds of checks and the criticisms.',
  3: 'This theory says every society moves through stages as it develops: first both births and deaths are high, then deaths fall while births stay high (population explodes), and finally both are low. Place India on that path.',
  4: 'Tribe questions are match-the-pair questions: tribe with State or region. Learn them State by State from the handout list.',
  5: 'A settlement is where people live together. <b>Rural</b> ones depend on land-based work and are classed by how close the houses are and by the pattern they make; <b>urban</b> ones are classed by size and function, with their own set of terms.',
 },
 6: {
  0: 'Indian farming runs on three seasons set by the monsoon: <b>kharif</b> (sown with the rains), <b>rabi</b> (sown in winter) and <b>zaid</b> (the short summer gap). Know which crops belong to which.',
  1: 'Each crop needs its own temperature, rainfall and soil. Read the table across: once the conditions are known, the growing States follow.',
  2: 'Farming types are sorted by <b>purpose and intensity</b>: for the family or for the market, with much labour on small plots or with machines on large ones. Shifting, plantation and mixed farming are the special cases.',
  3: 'Each “revolution” is a colour tied to one product. Learn them as pairs.',
  4: 'World agricultural regions follow climate. For each region hold where it is, how farming is done there and what is produced.',
  5: 'These are definition questions: one clean line for each term is enough.',
  7: 'A resource is anything that can be used to meet a need. They are classed by origin, by whether they renew, by ownership and by stage of development. Mining is how the mineral ones are taken out.',
  8: 'Minerals are tested as <b>mineral with State or mine</b>, and ore with metal. Learn them belt by belt.',
  9: 'Fossil fuels form from buried organic matter. For coal learn the <b>ranks</b> by carbon content and the fields; for oil and gas the basins; shale gas is gas trapped in fine-grained rock that needs fracturing to release.',
  10: 'Non-conventional sources are renewable: sun, wind, biomass, the Earth’s heat, tides and ocean temperature difference. For each know how it works in a line and where it is found; nuclear is taken alongside.',
  11: 'Industry is classed by raw material, size and ownership. Where an industry settles depends on a few pulls: <b>raw material, power, labour, market, transport and policy</b>. Then learn the main industrial regions.',
  12: 'Land transport: roads for flexibility and short hauls, railways for bulk over long distance, pipelines for liquids and gas. Learn the classification and the named networks.',
  13: 'Water transport is the cheapest for heavy, bulky goods. Two parts: <b>inland</b> waterways on rivers and canals, and <b>ocean</b> routes with their ports.',
  14: 'Air transport is the fastest and costliest, best for difficult terrain and long distances.',
  15: 'Communication moved from post and telegraph to telephone, radio, television, then digital and satellite links. Learn each with its key facts.',
 },
}
